Subject: [PATCH v8 0/6] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay jail
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev

Agents need to send and read p7x.dev mail without holding the
Fastmail credentials. This series builds lab-mail, the relay jail
that holds them, and points lab-claude's mail clients at it.

Patch 1 is the spec. Patches 2 to 5 build lab-mail one service at a
time: the jail and its credential mount, smtprelay for sending,
mbsync for the append-only copy of the lists folder, and Dovecot to
serve that copy read-only over IMAP. Patch 6 sets up msmtp, mbsync
and a separate notmuch profile in lab-claude, so the lab-devel list
keeps its jj maildir.

Testing. None of the services has run. smtprelay, mbsync, msmtp and
Dovecot are not installed in lab-claude, and I cannot build or start
a jail from here, so the acceptance test in the spec is still to do
on beastie. What I did check here: the userinfo script in patch 3
percent-encodes a login and password containing a space, "@", ":",
"/", "#", "?", "%" and a UTF-8 letter, and fails with a message on a
line with no space or an empty file; the two sed commands in patch 4
split the same line into the login and the full password.

That smtprelay decodes the percent-escapes is inference, not tested:
it parses its remote with Go's net/url, whose Userinfo returns
decoded values.

Changes in v8:
- The spec names the credentials file mail-creds, not creds, as Pat
  asked. Nothing else in the spec changed.
- The implementation follows the v7 spec, where the earlier posting
  of patches 2 to 6 followed an older one. The README, smtprelay and
  mbsync read the one mail-creds line in place of separate user and
  password files.
- smtprelay's remote is built by a new script, userinfo, which
  percent-encodes the login and password. The earlier version put
  them in the URL raw, so a password with "@", ":", "/" or "#" broke
  it, against the spec's "the password is the rest of the line".
